+\subsubsection{Operator definitions and relation to prior theory}\label{app:operator-crosswalk}
+For fixed training data and $W$, distinguish
+\[
+\Umat^{(1)}_k=\frac1\nsamp\sum_\mu\phi(x_t^{\mu,k})\phi(x_t^{\mu,k})^\top,\qquad
+\Umat^{(K)}=\frac1K\sum_{k=1}^K\Umat^{(1)}_k,\qquad
+\Umat=\E_\xi\Umat^{(1)}_k.
+\]
+Exactly, $\operatorname{rank}\Umat^{(1)}_k\le\min(\pwidth,\nsamp)$ and $\operatorname{rank}\Umat^{(K)}\le\min(\pwidth,K\nsamp)$. The first has at least $\pwidth-\nsamp$ zeros when $\pwidth>\nsamp$, with equality only at full row rank. Neither bound forces $\pwidth-\nsamp$ zeros in the expectation. The released \texttt{compute\_U} uses $K=50$ by default; the fixed-$q$ control uses $K=40$. Both estimate $\Umat$, but their eigenvalues are finite-Monte-Carlo quantities. Fresh-noise SGD has the mean Euler recursion of \cref{prop:sgdmean-p1}; its mean loss also contains parameter-variance terms and need not equal the loss of the mean iterate.
+
+An exact decomposition follows from conditional covariance. With $m_\mu=\E_\xi\phi(x_t^\mu)$,
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:conditional-U}
+\begin{aligned}
+&\Umat=B+C,\\ & B=\frac1\nsamp\sum_\mu m_\mu m_\mu^\top,\\ & C=\frac1\nsamp\sum_\mu\operatorname{Cov}_\xi(\phi(x_t^\mu)),\\ & B,C\succeq0,\quad\operatorname{rank}B\le\nsamp.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+This gives interlacing bounds relative to $C$, but no constant floor unless further structure of $C$ is established. Thus the cuts at $\dint,\dlat,\nsamp$ are reporting conventions for $\Umat^{(K)}$, not dimensions of invariant signal/sample subspaces proved by rank alone.
